    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**In a vacuum, it is a standard baseline offer for Google L6, but given your current position, it is mediocre.**\n\n* **Base Salary is a step down:** You would drop from $240,000 to $235,000, even though the posted range goes up to $271,000.\n* **Marginal Total Comp Increase:** Your annualized year-over-year compensation only rises from **~$526,000 to ~$552,000** ($235k base + $47k target bonus + $270k/yr annualized equity). A ~5% increase does not justify the friction and risk of changing companies.\n* **No Sign-on Bonus:** A $0 sign-on bonus leaves money on the table, especially when you are forfeiting accrued bonus timing and vesting continuity at Meta.\n* **The \"Refresher Stacking\" Pitch is Not Unique:** Meta also issues annual refreshers that stack over time. Comparing Google\u2019s projected future refreshes against your *current static* Meta compensation is an unequal comparison.\n\nBecause you were passively recruited and are happy where you are, this initial offer does not provide enough economic incentive to move.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Recommendation: Negotiate Firmly (Do Not Accept As-Is)\n\n**Do not accept, and do not walk away yet.** \n\nYou hold the strongest possible negotiation leverage:\n1. You were sourced passively and did not seek the job.\n2. You have a stable, well-compensated role at Meta (IC6).\n3. You have a concrete walk-away point because you are happy where you are.\n\nGoogle almost never leads with their top-of-band offer for L6, especially on equity and sign-on bonuses. They expect a counteroffer.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (and Exact Targets)\n\nTarget an annualized package that represents a real **15% to 20%+ step-up** over your current compensation, plus a one-time bridge:\n\n| Component | Current Meta | Initial Google Offer | Your Counter / Ask | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Closes in on the $271k top-of-band and ensures a base increase. |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **~$51,000 \u2013 $52,000** | Rises automatically with higher base (20%). |\n| **4-Year RSUs** | ~$1,000,000 ($250k/yr) | $1,080,000 ($270k/yr) | **$1,300,000 \u2013 $1,400,000** | Averages **$325k \u2013 $350k/yr** in equity. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Bridges unvested equity/bonus timing and transition risk. |\n| **Target Year 1 TC** | ~$526,000 | ~$552,000 | **~$630,000 \u2013 $660,000** | Includes sign-on; annualized run rate of ~$630k+. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Execute the Negotiation\n\n#### A. Clarify the Equity Vesting Schedule\nAsk the recruiter whether the $1.08M grant vests evenly (25% per year) or follows Google's front-loaded schedule (e.g., 33%/33%/22%/12% or 38%/32%/20%/10%). Front-loading increases your Year 1 cash flow, but you still want the overall 4-year grant value increased to avoid a steep cliff in years 3 and 4.\n\n#### B. The Framing / Script\nPosition yourself as genuinely excited about the role and team, but clear that leaving a strong setup at Meta requires a compensation package that reflects the step up in scope and transition risk:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team and I'm excited about the scope of the role. That said, as you know, I wasn't actively looking to move. At Meta, my current total compensation is around $526k, with a $240k base and meaningful unvested equity.*\n>\n> *For me to walk away from my current trajectory and take on the transition risk, the compensation needs to clearly represent a step forward. Specifically:*\n>\n> 1. * **Base:** To ensure I\u2019m not taking a step back on fixed compensation, I\u2019d like to see the base salary at **$255,000\u2013$260,000**, which sits comfortably within your posted range.\n> 2. * **Equity:** I\u2019d like to bring the 4-year RSU grant to **$1.35M** to make the ongoing equity meaningful relative to my Meta grants.\n> 3. * **Sign-on:** To offset the disruption to my unvested equity schedule and upcoming bonus cycle, I\u2019m looking for a **$75,000\u2013$100,000 sign-on bonus**.\n>\n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Summary\n* **Do not accept** the initial $552k package\u2014it is virtually flat against your Meta comp.\n* **Push on all three levers** (Base to $255k+, RSUs to $1.3M+, Sign-on to $75k+).\n* If Google meets you in the middle (e.g., ~$250k base, ~$1.25M equity, ~$50k sign-on), you will be looking at **~$610k+ in Year 1**, which makes the transition financially worthwhile. If they refuse to move, stay at Meta.",
