     "content": "### The Financial Breakdown\n\nThis is a **meaningful step up in Year 1**, and Google effectively called your bluff in the best way possible by offering a **$200,000 cash sign-on**. \n\nBecause they gave you $200k in cash *and* your full Year 1 Google equity grant, you are not just replacing your forfeited Meta equity\u2014**you are essentially double-dipping on that $200k in Year 1.**\n\nHere is what your compensation actually looks like:\n\n#### Year 1 Total Comp\n* **Base:** $240,000\n* **Target Bonus (20%):** $48,000\n* **Sign-on Bonus:** $200,000\n* **Year 1 RSUs:**\n  * *If flat vesting (25%/yr):* $270,000 $\\rightarrow$ **Total Year 1 = $758,000**\n  * *If standard Google front-loaded vesting (33% or 38% Year 1):* $356,000 \u2013 $410,000 $\\rightarrow$ **Total Year 1 = $844,000 \u2013 $898,000**\n* **Compared to Meta:** You make **+$232,000 to +$372,000 more** in cash and stock in your first 12 months.\n\n---\n\n#### Years 2\u20134 Total Comp (The Run-Rate Reality)\n* **Base + Bonus:** $288,000\n* **Base RSU Vesting (without refreshers):** ~$270,000/yr (or less in Years 3\u20134 if front-loaded)\n* **Year 2+ Baseline:** **~$558,000** (plus any refreshers you accumulate)\n* **Compared to Meta:** Your steady-state run-rate after Year 1 is roughly **+$30k\u2013$40k/yr higher** than your current Meta comp ($526k), assuming performance and refreshers track similarly.\n\n---\n\n### Should You Take It?\n\n#### **Take the offer if:**\n1. **You want the immediate cash/equity windfall:** Taking an extra ~$230k\u2013$370k in Year 1 is guaranteed liquidity that compounds significantly if invested.\n2. **You want Google on your resume / diversification:** Having both Meta and Google at the Senior PM (L6 / IC6) level makes you universally marketable across big tech and pre-IPO startups for future Director/VP roles.\n3. **You are open to a culture change:** Google generally offers better work-life balance and less operational thrash than Meta, though product velocity is often slower.\n\n#### **Stay at Meta if:**\n1. **You are on a near-term track for IC7 (Staff PM):** \n   * Meta promotes to IC7 significantly faster and with less bureaucracy than Google promotes from L6 $\\rightarrow$ L7. \n   * Moving to Google resets your social capital, internal network, and promotion clock back to zero for at least 1.5\u20132 years.\n2. **You genuinely love your current team/scope:** A ~$30k/yr steady-state increase after Year 1 is not life-changing once the sign-on bonus is behind you. If you leave a high-impact, high-visibility team for an unproven org at Google, the transition risk might not be worth it.\n\n---\n\n### Key Terms to Verify Before Signing\n\nIf you decide to accept, review these two fine-print items with the recruiter:\n\n1. **Sign-On Payment Schedule & Clawback:**\n   * Is the $200k paid lump-sum on your first paycheck, or split over 2 years (e.g., $100k Year 1 / $100k Year 2)?\n   * Ensure the clawback clause is **pro-rated** (e.g., if you leave at month 10, you only owe back 2/12ths, not the full 100%).\n2. **Vesting Cadence:**\n   * Confirm the exact vesting schedule (Google typically vests monthly after an initial period, and many grants follow front-loaded schedules like 33%/33%/22%/12% or 38%/32%/20%/10%).",
